    body: "Two primers finish the routing quartet. The IS-IS primer explains the twin with the strange accent: why it runs directly over layer 2 and cannot be attacked from three hops away, the NET address ritual, levels instead of areas with the backbone as a contiguous set rather than a configured zone, the preemptive DIS election that catches OSPF natives, and the TLV design that absorbed IPv6, traffic engineering, and Segment Routing without ever needing a version break - extensibility as the feature that outlives every feature. The MPLS primer fits the whole mechanism into three verbs - push, swap, pop - then builds the architecture from the label stack: the BGP-free core, RFC 4364 L3VPNs with a thousand overlapping 10.0.0.0/8 customers never meeting, the LDP-to-RSVP-TE-to-Segment-Routing signaling arc, penultimate hop popping, the four-byte MTU tax, and an honest two-half answer to the SD-WAN question. The glossary gains the multicast wing - IGMP raising hands at the last hop with snooping as the melted-network preventive, PIM building shared and source trees around rendezvous points, and DVMRP as the flood-and-prune pioneer whose MBone streamed the Rolling Stones before streaming had a name - while the IS-IS and MPLS entries themselves received full house-depth rewrites. Glossary: 1,085 entries; the Learn shelf: 239 articles.",
